.TH tuprovider-ftp n 0.2 tclutils
.SH tclutils::tuprovider::ftp
.PP
An FTP backend for the \fBtuprovider\fR interface, built on the tcllib \fBftp\fR client.
.PP
It lets the same tree and list consumers that work over the local filesystem work over a remote FTP server without change: for the consumer, an FTP path is just a path with children.
.SS Load
.CS
tcl::tm::path add /path/to/tclutils/lib/tm
package require tclutils::tuprovider::ftp
.CE
.PP
Loading the package registers the \fBftp\fR scheme with \fBtuprovider\fR.
.SS Open
.CS
set p [::tclutils::tuprovider open ftp ftp://host/pub -user me -password secret]
.CE
.PP
The URL is \fBftp://host[:port][/base]\fR. Options after it go to \fBftp::Open\fR: \fB\-user\fR, \fB\-password\fR, \fB\-port\fR, \fB\-mode passive|active\fR, \fB\-timeout\fR. Anonymous is the default. A \fB/base\fR in the URL is entered with \fBCD\fR after connecting.
.SS Capabilities
.PP
\fBftp\fR reports the full read/write set FTP supports:
.CS
list stat get put delete mkdir move
.CE
.PP
It does \fBnot\fR report \fBcopy\fR: FTP has no server\-side copy. To copy a file, read it with \fBget\fR and write it back with \fBput\fR (a cross\-provider copy does exactly this at the application level). \fBmove\fR maps to the FTP \fBRENAME\fR command.
.SS Operations
.IP \(bu
\fBlist $path\fR \-\- parses the server's \fBLIST\fR output into entry dicts. The common Unix listing format is assumed (\fBperms links owner group size date name\fR); the leading char of the perms column marks a directory. Names with spaces are kept whole.
.IP \(bu
\fBstat $path\fR \-\- derives dir/file by trying to \fBCD\fR into the path (dir if it succeeds), with \fBFileSize\fR for files.
.IP \(bu
\fBget\fR / \fBput\fR / \fBdelete\fR / \fBmkdir\fR / \fBmove\fR \-\- forward to the matching ftp commands.
.SS "Notes and limits"
.IP \(bu
\fBLIST is not standardized.\fR This adapter parses the common Unix format; a server emitting a different format (some Windows FTP servers) may not parse cleanly. \fBNLST\fR\-only servers are not handled.
.IP \(bu
Paths are absolute FTP paths; the consumer treats them as opaque.
.IP \(bu
The FTP traffic itself is tcllib's concern. What this adapter adds is the LIST parsing, the path \-> entry mapping, and the honest \fBcaps\fR set.
.SS "See also"
.PP
\fBtuprovider\fR, \fBtuprovider::dav\fR, \fBtuprovider::zip\fR
